{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 16,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.069,
            0.144,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "84"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.488,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.107,
            0.852,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "This gives us, $( a + b ) ^ { 3 } = ( a + b ) ( a ^ { 2 } + 2 a b + b ^ { 2 } ) = a ^ { 3 } + 3 a ^ { 2 }   b + 3 a b ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 3 } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.137,
            0.632,
            0.157
          ],
          "content": "Here we have found a new identity, namely,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.162,
            0.672,
            0.184
          ],
          "content": "(a+b)^{3}=a^{3}+3a^{2}b+3ab^{2}+b^{3}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.188,
            0.836,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": "What happens when we replace b with – b in this new identity?"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.303,
            0.218,
            0.728,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": "\\left[ a + ( - b ) \\right] ^ { 3 } = a ^ { 3 } + 3 a ^ { 2 } ( - b ) + 3 a ( - b ) ^ { 2 } + ( - b ) ^ { 3 } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.249,
            0.885,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": "This leads to the identity $(a - b)^{3} = a^{3} - 3a^{2}b + 3ab^{2} - b^{3}$ .     Note that out of the four terms, two are positive and two are negative. They appear alternately in the expression."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.309,
            0.666,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "Let us see how we can use these new identities."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.333,
            0.884,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": "**Example 13:** What is the side of the cube whose volume is $p ^ { 3 } + 6 p ^ { 2 } q + 1 2 p q ^ { 2 } + 8 q ^ { 3 }$ cubic units?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.374,
            0.884,
            0.424
          ],
          "content": "Comparing $p ^ { 3 } + 6 p ^ { 2 } q + 1 2 p q ^ { 2 } + 8 q ^ { 3 }$ with the right side of the identity $(a+b)^{3}=a^{3}+3a^{2}b+3ab^{2}+b^{3}$ , we may rewrite it as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.431,
            0.5,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": "( p ) ^ { 3 } + 3   ( p ) ^ { 2 } ( 2 q ) + 3 ( p ) ( 2 q ) ^ { 2 } + ( 2 q ) ^ { 3 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.459,
            0.875,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "which is $( p + 2 q ) ^ { 3 }$ , so that $a = p$ and $b = 2 q$ . Hence a side of the cube will be $p + 2 q$ units."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.512,
            0.616,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": "**Example 14:** Now consider the expression"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.337,
            0.536,
            0.665,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": "8n^{3}-60n^{2}m+150nm^{2}-125m^{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.561,
            0.751,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": "If you write it in the form $( a - b ) ^ { 3 } ,$ what will be a and b?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.587,
            0.778,
            0.607
          ],
          "content": "Rewriting the expression $8n^{3}-60n^{2}m+150nm^{2}-125m^{3}$ as"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.61,
            0.799,
            0.633
          ],
          "content": "$( 2 n ) ^ { 3 } - 3 ( 2 n ) ^ { 2 } ( 5 m ) + 3 ( 2 n ) ( 5 m ) ^ { 2 } - ( 5 m ) ^ { 3 }$ and comparing it with"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.638,
            0.884,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": "$a^{3}-3a^{2}b+3ab^{2}-b^{3}=(a-b)^{3}$ , we get $( 2 n \\mathrm { ~ - ~ } 5 m ) ^ { 3 }$ . Hence $a = 2 n$ and $b = 5 m$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.684,
            0.883,
            0.726
          ],
          "content": "Now let us play with known identities to discover more identities. Try to multiply the following using the distributive property."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.733,
            0.368,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "1. $(x - y)(x^{2} + xy + y^{2})$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.756,
            0.368,
            0.777
          ],
          "content": "2. $(x + y)(x^2 - xy + y^2)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.782,
            0.625,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": "Let us work out the product of the first one."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.809,
            0.792,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}(x - y)(x^{2} + xy + y^{2}) &= x^{3} + x^{2}y + xy^{2} - x^{2}y - xy^{2} - y^{3} \\\\&= x^{3} + x^{2}y + xy^{2} - x^{2}y - xy^{2} - y^{3} = x^{3} - y^{3}.\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_23.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
